{"definition":"A rolled-up document or scroll, the standard ancient form for recording and reading written texts.","senseLabels":["Written scroll or roll"],"article":"מְגִלָּה means a scroll — a document in rolled form, derived from the root גָּלַל (to roll). Its 21 occurrences cluster heavily in Jeremiah (14 times), reflecting the extended narrative of Jeremiah's dictated scroll that was publicly read and then burned by King Jehoiakim. The LXX's primary rendering κεφαλίς (scroll or book-roll) confirms this straightforward meaning, while Zechariah's vision of a flying scroll and Ezekiel's eating of a written scroll show the word also appears in symbolic prophetic imagery.","caution":"The unusual LXX renderings δρέπανον (sickle) for Zechariah's flying scroll and scattered other Greek choices suggest occasional translator uncertainty about the word's referent in visionary contexts."}
